# Auth sessions / captured credentials — never commit
sites/*/.session/
data/

# Local agent tooling state — not part of the repo
.agents/
.opencode/
skills-lock.json

# Generated per-site servers are build output of the workflow.
#
# GOAL 142: `sites/*/server/` did NOT cover the directory that actually exists.
# A run that targets `sites/` directly (rather than `sites/<host>/`) wrote
# `sites/server/`, which the pattern cannot match, so six generated files were
# COMMITTED — including an ACP server embedding this machine's absolute path
# (`/home/me/.../src/runtime/browser-session.ts`). Build output that carries a
# machine-specific path must never be tracked. Both shapes are ignored now.
sites/*/server/
sites/server/

# Dependencies / build
#
# GOAL 150: `.ci-verdict/` is per-RUN STATE — the machine-readable record of
# whether the browser-dependent half of the suite actually ran, stamped with the
# CI job id that produced it. Committing it would ship a claim about one run on
# one machine as if it were a property of the repository, and a checked-in
# verdict is exactly the "silently absent coverage that reads as green" defect
# in a new disguise: it would satisfy a gate without any browser being launched.
.ci-verdict/

# Secrets — the credential red line.
#
# CRITICAL, found by CI (pipeline 193) and NOT by me: `.env` was NEVER ignored by
# this repository. It only appeared ignored on the operator's box because of a
# PERSONAL `~/.gitignore_global` — so the protection was an accident of one
# machine, and a clean runner (CI, a fresh clone, any contributor) had NO
# protection at all. A repo whose own ignore file does not exclude `.env` cannot
# honestly claim the red line is machine-verified: `test/credential-red-line`
# measures with `git check-ignore`, and it passed locally for the wrong reason.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

node_modules/
dist/
*.log
*.tgz

# Local planning docs (intentionally not published)
.plans/

# GOAL 125: a captured action map can contain live session credentials
# (a --login capture records the site's own auth POSTs), and the file is a
# tracked class by default. Redaction is the real fix; this is the backstop.
sites/*/action-map.json
sites/action-map.json
sites/*/*/action-map.json
